sections.body:
A good PWS has:
- measurable performance standards (what 'good' looks like: timeliness, accuracy, quality);
- an acceptable quality level when perfection is not required;
- a **quality assurance surveillance plan (QASP)** telling the Government how it will check performance (DFARS 237.872 for DoW service contracts);
- positive or negative incentives where useful.

- 6.103-1: only one responsible source and nothing else will meet the need (also the authority for brand-name requirements);
- 6.103-2: unusual and compelling urgency;
- 6.103-3: industrial mobilization, engineering or research capability, or expert services;
- 6.103-4: international agreement;
- 6.103-5: authorized or required by statute (for example 8(a) sole source);
- 6.103-6: national security;
- 6.103-7: public interest.

Using an exception usually needs a written justification with the required content, including the contracting officer's certification (FAR 6.104-1). Approval depends on the dollar value. For DoW, the levels are in DFARS 206.104-71, Table 206-1:
- $10 million or below: the contracting officer;
- over $10 million up to $100 million: the competition advocate;
- over $100 million up to $500 million: the head of the contracting activity;
- over $500 million: the senior procurement executive.

The justification is generally made public after award (FAR 6.301). Note: the governmentwide FAR table (6.104-2) has different numbers; DoW uses its own table.

Start as soon as the need is known, with the customer, contracting, finance, legal and the small business professional (FAR 7.104). A plan covers the whole life of the buy (FAR 7.102):
- the need, its history and any constraints;
- cost, schedule and performance goals;
- market research results and sources;
- competition and small business strategy;
- contract type and method;
- risks and how to reduce them;
- milestones, including how long the contracting work will take.
